  "grammar_explanations": [
    {
      "slug": "can/can't: ability and permission",
      "title_form": "Can y Can't: Habilidad y Permiso",
      "title_target": "Can and Can't: Ability and Permission",
      "description_md_form": "**Can** es un verbo especial que usamos para hablar de **habilidades** (lo que sabemos hacer) y para **pedir permiso**. Es igual para todas las personas: *I can, you can, he can, she can*... ¡No cambia!\n\n- **Afirmativo:** *I can sing.* (Yo puedo cantar.)\n- **Negativo:** *I can't dance.* (No puedo bailar.)\n- **Pregunta:** *Can I play music?* (¿Puedo poner música?)\n\nDespués de **can** o **can't**, el otro verbo va **sin \"to\"** y sin cambios: *I can run*, no *I can to run*. Para preguntar, ponemos **can** al inicio. Para responder, usamos *Yes, you can* o *No, you can't*.",
      "description_md_target": "**Can** is a special verb we use to talk about **abilities** (things we know how to do) and to **ask for permission**. It is the same for all people: *I can, you can, he can, she can*... It doesn't change!\n\n- **Affirmative:** *I can sing.*\n- **Negative:** *I can't dance.*\n- **Question:** *Can I play music?*\n\nAfter **can** or **can't**, the other verb goes **without \"to\"** and without changes: *I can run*, not *I can to run*. To ask, we put **can** at the start. To answer, we use *Yes, you can* or *No, you can't*.",
      "grammar_structure": "Subject + can/can't + base verb",
      "conjugation_table": {
        "name": "can_present",
        "headers": [
          "Forma",
          "Ejemplo"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "Afirmativo",
            "I can cook."
          ],
          [
            "Negativo",
            "I can't swim."
          ],
          [
            "Pregunta",
            "Can I jump?"
          ],
          [
            "Respuesta corta sí",
            "Yes, you can."
          ],
          [
            "Respuesta corta no",
            "No, you can't."
          ]
        ]
      },
